# Secrets. Never commit these. The hub's tokens and the database URL live here and nowhere else.
.env
.env.local
.env*.local

# The CLI's per-machine config: contains the agent token.
.command-center/

node_modules/
.next/
out/
build/
dist/
*.log
.DS_Store
.vercel
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Local scratch from the proof runs
tests/.out/

.vercel

# Everything env-shaped is a secret BY DEFAULT — a new `.env.something` should be ignored without anyone
# having to remember to add it. This line is why `CC_ALLOW_FAULT_INJECTION` and a production connection
# string have never reached a commit.
.env*

# ...EXCEPT the example, which is the one env file that must ship.
#
# THIS LINE WAS MISSING AND IT BROKE THE FIRST COMMAND OF THE SETUP GUIDE. `.env*` above matched
# `.env.example`, so it had never been committed — and README's opening instruction is
# `cp .env.example .env.local`, which on a fresh clone fails with "No such file or directory". Nobody had
# executed the first step of their own setup path from a clean checkout; on the machine that wrote it, the
# file was simply there, untracked. Verified by cloning this repository into a temporary directory and
# looking, which is the only way that class of defect is ever found.
#
# It contains no values, only variable names and the reasons for them. It is checked by nothing automatic,
# so: if you add a variable the hub reads, it goes in there.
!.env.example

# Screenshots are regenerable (npm run shots), but the "before" set cannot be
# regenerated once the interface changes, so tests/shots IS committed on purpose.

# The permission relay's own switch, and it must stay per-clone.
#
# `cc approvals on` writes .claude/settings.json with a PermissionRequest hook. docs/SETUP.md promises the
# relay is "off in every project until somebody runs `cc approvals on` in that folder" — so committing this
# file would make that sentence false for everyone who clones, and would hand a fresh clone a hook that lets
# whoever holds the web token answer tool calls. Found before publishing, by checking what git would take:
# the file was untracked and unignored, which is one `git add -A` away from shipping behaviour nobody chose.
#
# It carries no token, so this is about DEFAULTS rather than secrecy. A team that genuinely wants a shared
# relay can force-add it and delete this line, deliberately.
.claude/
